        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jeysie/{size}/1868_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-12-01T02:54:14.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I know in one of the past episodes C&amp;A explicitly said Biomancer doesn't raise the dead, so I don't think that particular factor comes up. (Whether he can or would want to I don't think was addressed, though.)</p><p>Since I've mentioned all the others already, the last question I asked that didn't come up elsewhere was why the term \"flesh<em>children</em>\" exactly, and what that says about Biomancer in terms of intended connotations.</p><p>It occurs to me that Biomancer is also a foil to Unity in some ways, in addition to the Scholar. She has her army of metallic \"friends\" to keep her company made from manipulating metal that she sometimes uses to mimic and tease people and he has his cadre of \"children\" to keep him company made from manipulating flesh that he sometimes uses to mimic and mock people.</p>",
        "post_number": 27,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:39:41.331Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
